    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d643">

                <pb facs="0724" n="714" sameAs="frus:frus1943China/pg714"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.9331/257</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Adviser on Political Relations</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hornbeck</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Commercial Secretary of the British Embassy</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Jopson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-07-05">July 5, 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Jopson</hi>: I acknowledge receipt of your
                    letter of June 23 regarding exchange of information in connection with questions
                    of policy and action relating to conclusion with China of treaties of
                    commerce.</p>
                <p>It has been the understanding of officers of the Department who were parties to
                    the conversation held on May 7 [<hi rendition="#italic">6</hi>] that there was
                    reached a consensus of opinion that the matters then discussed might most
                    appropriately and to greatest advantage be dealt with informally. As was then
                    indicated, we share with you the desire and hope that the British Government and
                    the American Government will keep each other informed of any significant
                    developments in relation to those matters. We are glad to have now the
                    information given in your letter under acknowledgment. So far as this Government
                    is concerned there have been since the moment of the discussion under reference
                    no new developments.</p>
                <p>Yours sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stanley K. Hornbeck</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
